 "overview": {
  "summary": "Every auxiliary interface of the instrument on one board across the back of the chassis. An unguarded half, referenced to +5 LOGIC COMMON, holds the CPU interface (bus transceiver U1, address decoder PLD U8, the 3.6864 MHz clock regenerator U18), the IEEE-488 interface (TMS9914 U2 with 75160/75162 buffers U3 and U4 to connector J1), and the RS-232-C interface (68C681 DUART U5 with 1488 driver U6 and 1489 receiver U7 to J2). A guarded half, referenced to LH COM, holds an 82C55 (U14) and two 5801 relay drivers (U16, U17) on the guarded digital bus from J8 that control the board's twelve relays plus K12 on the analog motherboard. The relays route the 5725A boost amplifier signals between J8 and J7 (K1, K2, K6-K9), switch the PHASE LOCK IN (J6) and VARIABLE PHASE OUT (J5) BNCs (K10, K12), and handle the 5205A/5220A amplifier interfaces on J3 and J4 through latches U9/U10 and buffers U11/U12. The DUART's second channel is the 5725A's digital control link.",
  "sections": "§2-21, §2-204 … §2-214",
  "note": "Nothing on the board is measured by the calibrator's own adc, so the only fault codes are the 82C55 and DUART register tests, 3700-3705 and 3708. Everything else -- a dead GPIB, a silent serial port, a 5725A that will not talk -- presents as a remote-interface symptom and is worked from the theory and the sheets. Table 2-16 (VCO frequencies) belongs to the phase-locked loop on A13, which this board only feeds."
 },
